Great question — and it's actually a really smart concern! Here's the problem with formula-based lookups:

⚠️ If you delete rows from Sheet 5, any formula in Sheet 2 pointing to those deleted rows will break or return errors.

✅ The Better Approach: VBA Macro (Submit Button)
Instead of live formulas, use a macro-triggered copy-paste as values approach:

How it works:


Sheet 1 (Form) → User enters Route ID & hits Submit
        ↓
VBA Macro fires → Looks up Route ID in Sheet 5
        ↓
Copies matching data → Pastes as STATIC VALUES into Sheet 2 (new row)
        ↓
Sheet 5 data can now be safely deleted ✅
Sheet 2 retains the data permanently ✅
Why this is better:
Approach	Safe to delete Sheet 5?	Dynamic?
VLOOKUP / XLOOKUP	❌ Breaks	✅ Live
VBA Macro (paste as values)	✅ Safe	✅ Logged permanently
Power Query	❌ Loses data	✅ Refreshable
🏆 This also gives you a bonus benefit:
Sheet 2 effectively becomes a permanent issue log — every submission is stamped and recorded, even if the original route data in Sheet 5 is later modified or deleted. This is ideal for a logistics tracker where you need audit history.

So the plan would be:

Sheet 1 → Submit button triggers a VBA macro
Macro → Fetches from Sheet 5, pastes values into Sheet 2
Sheet 5 → Can be freely cleaned/archived
